#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82820104-bd75-549b-84e9-d225a7f4616b" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous ODE to a First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "3819a294-7975-5c5c-b3d8-100032d5cd33" :question/math-academy-id "e-9040" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\nx_{1}^{′} = y^{′}; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3},; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$, and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 4t^{2}x_{1} - t^{3}x_{2} + 3\\sqrt{t}x_{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166633 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/id #uuid "529c6004-c3f2-5e7a-8a04-8fff4f886ab8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9041" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n$y'' + y' - 6y= 0, \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = q$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p-q)\\cdot f(t)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\\ny^{′}(0) &= x_{2}(0) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' =-y' + 6y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' =-x_2 + 6x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = 6x_{1} - x_{2},\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = 4\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p - q) \\cdot f(t) &= (1 - 4)(6x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-3(6x_{1} - x_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "e81f0b0b-985f-5e01-b821-fde2aa8bc7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9138" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the matrix $A$." :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 2x_{1} - 5x_{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79bb2fe-b1ab-5545-b51f-6dfa50fd6b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163389} {:answer/id #uuid "b85b6a7c-695a-593a-8228-839bd1d88776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x_{1} + 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163390} {:answer/id #uuid "2aec8325-b896-5946-a1a3-b5fef93e5f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163391} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6ece57-ea87-58f1-9e69-1e24a88cb866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2x_{1} - 3x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163392} {:answer/id #uuid "7df1dd86-c1f3-584e-a95e-239282bc37c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x_{1} - 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7df1dd86-c1f3-584e-a95e-239282bc37c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x_{1} - 3x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163393} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a03a451-d462-541c-bf05-614cba58c578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163388}] :question/id #uuid "80a38704-3722-5fd2-9d30-5b217f5c3710" :question/math-academy-id "q-107906" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) = 3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(1) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(1) = q$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q) \\cdot f(t)$."}]}
